A||||6||HummelHund||Car died, what happened? (fairly long)||||5||
Z||000000||HummelHund||08-26-2001||03:47 PM||||Last night while driving home my 84' 2.5 died on me. No warning, just poof!,it's dead. It took us about 20 min to gather our forces and push it from its resting place in the fast lane of a four lane highway, but we finally got it to safety. Suspecting the alternator, we switched batteries but the car didn't seem to want to start. We pushed it a little farther to a place more suited to sitting all night and gave it one more try, this time it started. Still suspecting it could be the alternator and not wanting to use the lights, we left it there for the night. We picked it up today, it started right up and ran well all the way home. Once we got back we left it running and tested the voltage to the battery and the alternator was putting out 14.5 volts with no accessories, and 13.1 volts with lights, wipers, A/C on high, and with me holding down the power window switches. So...what should I look into first? I've been in a similar situation in a Mercury Capri and it turned out to the the ignition module, and we haven't replaced that on the Fiero yet. Do the symptoms say ignition module, or maybe a loose connection, I'm open to any and all suggestions. Thanks in advance for any help y'all can give me!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe, The "Black Wedge of Death" (FOR SALE $400, RUNS)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mach10||08-26-2001||03:55 PM||||Coil or Ignition Module. Get your module checked out at a parts store for free. Don't know about the coil(s -- if you have a DIS-4)||||reg||3
Z||000002||HummelHund||08-26-2001||04:01 PM||||I didn't even consider the coil...I'll look into that. A while back I had the ignition module tested, and it tested good, but that's been a couple of months, I'll pull it off and take it to be tested again.<BR>Thanks and if anyone else has any guesses, keep em coming!<BR>And it's an old fashioned 84' Duke, no DIS, but the coil still seems like a likely suspect...<p>[This message has been edited by HummelHund (edited 08-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||08-26-2001||04:09 PM||||Your distributer could also be flocked...||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||08-26-2001||05:42 PM||||I'm not following this real well. You suspected the alt, why? Would it not turn over? or crank over but not start? On my '84 duke, the first time the ign mod quit, it just suddenly died, in the middle of nowhere.<BR>The last time, it was an intermitent thing. Run ok for a few miles, start acting up, then die until it cooled down. After cooldown, it would start fine and run for a while. I would suspect the ign mod in your case, but not rule out the coil.<BR>While I'm here on this sub, the first time I chngd the mod, there was a little square chip looking affair in the dist, about 3/8 X 3/8 with one wire on it. The wire was burned off right next to the chip. No auto store in town knew what it was so I put the new ign mod in without it. Been running fine ever since. Anybody know what this thing was? Something to dissapate ign noise from the radio maybe? Thx<BR>Don||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||08-26-2001||07:09 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000006||HummelHund||08-26-2001||08:13 PM||||We suspected the distributor because the car was turning over but not starting, and we had had a previous experience in a Grand Am that was similar. I will definitely get my ignition module tested again.<BR>By the way, what's a "flocked distributor"? I've never heard of that term, I'll have to add it to my Fiero vocabulary.<BR>||||reg||1
